Skip non-executable files silently instead of logging an error.

networkd-dispatcher scans every entry in each state directory and logs
"Unable to execute script, check file mode" for any file lacking the user
execute bit. That treats ordinary non-script files as errors: Portage's
keepdir marker (.keep_*), editor swap/backup files, READMEs dropped next to
hooks, etc. These are not broken hooks and the repeated error lines are noise
on every state transition.

Silently skip files that are not executable. Keep erroring on files that ARE
executable but not root-owned, since a non-root-owned executable hook is a
real security concern worth surfacing.

--- a/networkd-dispatcher
+++ b/networkd-dispatcher
@@ -170,10 +170,10 @@ def scripts_in_path(path, subdir):
 
             if os.path.isfile(pathname):
                 entry = os.stat(pathname)
-                # Make sure script can be executed
+                # Silently skip anything that is not executable: Portage
+                # .keep markers, editor swap/backup files, READMEs, etc.
                 if not stat.S_IXUSR & entry.st_mode:
-                    logger.error("Unable to execute script, check file mode: "
-                                 "%s", pathname)
+                    logger.debug("Skipping non-executable file: %s", pathname)
                 # Make sure script is owned by root
                 elif entry.st_uid != 0 or entry.st_gid != 0:
                     logger.error("Unable to execute script, check file perms: "
